### Data analysis

#### Column 1

::: {.card title="Analysis"}

**Drug Resistance**

Drug resistance mutations were identified using the Stanford HIVdb algorithm via [sierrapy](https://github.com/hivdb/sierra-client) (v0.4.3), querying the HIVdb database (vHIVDB_10.2 (2026-04-26)) with the Sierra web service (v3.5.7 (2026-09-27)) . High-level resistance (level 5) was retained. Mutations were then filtered down to Surveillance Drug Resistance Mutations (SDRMs) using the curated tables for each drug class, accessed on 13/03/2026 [@stanford_hivdb_sdrm].

**Clustering**

Pairwise genetic distances were computed under the TN93 model using [HIV-TRACE](https://github.com/veg/hivtrace) (>=0.9.2,<0.10) [@tamura_estimation_1993]. Sequences were aligned to the HXB2_pol reference and clustered below a distance threfhold of 0.03. Sequences were compared if they had a minimum pairwise overlap of 800 bp, and ambiguities were treated with the resolve option. Drug resistance mutations were stripped using the `lewis` option.

Potential contaminants (such as clustering with the reference) and spurious transitive edges are also removed using the `--curate` `remove` option and `--filter` `remove` options.

**Autotune**
The choice of clustering threshold was investigated using the autotune algorithm [@weaver_auto-tune_2024]. Pairwise distances were recomputed over thresholds from 0.0 to 0.05, yielding a score that balances the number and size of clusters. Higher scores indicate more even clustering, with values above 1.9 considered preferable. The threshold used for this report is indicated in [@fig-autotune], and if the optimal threshold diverges substantially from this value, then it may need revision.
:::

